RELEASE NOTES, c3p0-0.12.0
==========================

THIS IS A CRITICAL SECURITY RELEASE.

Please update immediately.

Unbeknownst to, and to the great shame of, the library author, c3p0 has been abused for
roughly a decade as a "deserialization gadget". Attackers who succeed at placing maliciously
crafted Java-serialized objects or javax.naming.Reference instances in places where applications
will unmarshall or dereference them have been able to misuse functionality in c3p0 and its
main dependency, mchange-commons-java, to download and execute malicious code.

For more information on the exploits, see for example

* https://mogwailabs.de/en/blog/2025/02/c3p0-you-little-rascal/
* https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mbechler/marshalsec/master/marshalsec.pdf

c3p0-0.12.0 addresses these issues. The library author believes these attacks
are now effectively mitigated, unless installations affirmatively configure
the library to support the dangerous behavior. (Very few installations should
do so.)

Please see https://www.mchange.com/projects/c3p0/#configuring_security for
full details.

Many thanks to David Pollak of Spice Labs for a very detailed report about this issue.

---

The library also addresses a rare inconsistency in the statement cache where
Statements intended to be removed from the cache were not, because they were
checked out.

Thanks to vimalesh on GitHub for reporting that issue.


